1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,733
A better question arrived as a message on a laptop in the middle of a long period of development: does this system enable a person to leave as easily as it enables them to join?

2
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:18,263
The group had drawn up a summary of the program, but nobody had written the exit line.

3
00:00:18,263 --> 00:00:26,671
The precise measurement of privacy turned out to be simple: the number of steps required to cancel an account.

4
00:00:26,671 --> 00:00:40,519
A summary with numerous footnotes, a brilliant formulation of the principle, and a heap of approvals from the main office were useless if the answer was forty-seven clicks on a tablet.

5
00:00:40,519 --> 00:00:45,023
The false assumption was that simplicity is a weakness.

6
00:00:45,023 --> 00:00:51,022
“Simplicity is not the superiority of the lazy,” the analyst said.

7
00:00:51,022 --> 00:01:07,840
“It is the only thing a slender budget can buy.” A vitamin company had asked for an attribute they could not explain, and a dating service had asked for a beverage sponsor: both wanted data they could not justify.

8
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:10,944
The paradox was mathematical.

9
00:01:10,944 --> 00:01:21,253
In arithmetic, a zero costs nothing; in privacy, a zero is the only permissible value for the data you do not need.

10
00:01:21,253 --> 00:01:30,491
A cardboard box, a torch, and a calculator on the desk made the point better than the geometry on the whiteboard.

11
00:01:30,491 --> 00:01:41,269
The consensus was to minimize, not to delete: every field would need an owner, and every owner would need to fold their data into one page.

12
00:01:41,269 --> 00:01:55,212
The elegance of the new form was that a user could read it aloud; the true test of a high-tech system is whether a microscopic line of password text can be explained by the person who typed it.

13
00:01:55,212 --> 00:02:03,676
The networking diagram had a shadow of its own: a fertilizer of small features that nobody had asked for.

14
00:02:03,676 --> 00:02:09,340
“We cannot solve a trust problem with an extra field,” Maya wrote.

15
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:18,688
“We can only stop adding them.” The next revision would be shorter than the previous one, and that would be the whole argument.

16
00:02:18,688 --> 00:02:28,582
It was difficult to see the manipulation in a form that looked so plain; the math was renewable, but an astronomical budget is not.

17
00:02:28,582 --> 00:02:40,651
The effort to get the product to the people who need it, to provide the music of a simple interface, and to reach them in time was the real design work.
